yesterday the federal reserve decided to  raise interest rates by 75 basis points  this is the second consecutive time that  they've raised the interest rate by such  an aggressive measure if we go back to  november of last year we had inflation  teeing around seven percent and the  federal reserve started to talk  aggressively saying that they needed to  destroy demand and bring the market back  into a more rational zone a lot of folks  didn't believe them but the federal  reserve has now done multiple interest  rate hikes and they've started to  conduct quantitative tightening in order  to make financial conditions something  that will bring the high inflation down  now raising interest rates by 75 basis  points is not something that they were  anticipating at the end of q1 of this  year but now here we are and they've  done it two different times if we look  at the coverage we can see that folks  are openly discussing that the federal  reserve is raising interest rates by 75  basis points specifically to tamp down  runaway inflation without creating a  recession  jerome powell had some choice words he  said that as the stance of monetary  policy tightens further it will likely  become appropriate to slow the pace of  increases while we assess how our  cumulative policy adjustments are  affecting the economy and inflation of  course this brings up the topic of a  recession and according to the fomc they  say that recent indicators of spending  and production have softened nonetheless  job gains have been robust in recent  months and the unemployment rate has  remained low jerome powell also  confirming that he does not believe we  are in a recession he said think what a  recession is it's a broad-based decline  across many industries that sustained  more than a couple of months this  doesn't seem like that now the real  reason is the labor market has been such  a strong signal of economic strength  that it makes you question the gdp data  now that is absolutely incredible to see  the chairman of the federal reserve  openly stating that he is questioning  the gdp data what does he know that we  don't know we're supposed to look at the  gdp data and have confidence in it but  we have the leader of the central bank  saying that maybe we shouldn't take it  at face value  regardless of what they say just look at  the data  two consecutive negative growth quarters  of gdp we are in a recession the federal  reserve was conducting asset purchases  and holding interest rates artificially  low when inflation was over seven  percent they finally realized they had  to destroy demand and tried to address  the inflation  and now we are in a fed induced  recession they have been hiking interest  rates as we move into that recession  sure employment is strong right now and  consumer spending is still growing but  we're seeing early signs hiring freezes  layoffs and consumers starting to  psychologically realize the recession is  on the horizon  over the coming months my guess is that  this data is going to turn and we will  see more and more data points confirming  that we in fact are in a recession the  federal reserve is going to have to  pivot they cannot continue to take an  aggressive quantitative tightening  stance  we have to get relief to the market or  else we risk going into a severe  recession and potentially a depression  now i don't think that that's likely to  happen because i think the fed will  pivot i think we will return to loose  monetary policy that is the macro  structure in which the economy is built  to survive on ultimately you just simply  have to survive the coming months until  the fed waves that white flag  loose monetary policy will make a return  in some cases it may come back with a  vengeance  until then the fed's in control it's all  one big trade and right now the u.s  economy is in a recession and until the  fed pivots we're not coming out of it
 

